1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,787
Take the mortgage from our first episode, and

2
00:00:02,787 --> 00:00:05,108
pay just one hundred pounds a month extra.

3
00:00:05,108 --> 00:00:07,263
It's gone three and a half years early,

4
00:00:07,263 --> 00:00:10,247
and you keep twenty-four thousand, five hundred pounds

5
00:00:10,247 --> 00:00:12,071
that would have gone on interest.

6
00:00:12,071 --> 00:00:15,608
Here's exactly how that happens, and whether it's right for you.

7
00:00:19,710 --> 00:00:21,883
In our first episode, we saw why a

8
00:00:21,883 --> 00:00:24,696
mortgage costs so much more than you borrow.

9
00:00:24,696 --> 00:00:27,252
Last time, we saw what a rate rise does.

10
00:00:27,252 --> 00:00:30,832
Today, the one lever you control: paying a little extra.

11
00:00:30,832 --> 00:00:33,964
We'll follow one hundred pounds a month from your

12
00:00:33,964 --> 00:00:36,904
bank account into the balance, and see what it

13
00:00:36,904 --> 00:00:39,781
does to your end date, your interest, and the

14
00:00:39,781 --> 00:00:42,529
moment your payments start working for you.

15
00:00:42,529 --> 00:00:45,597
As always, this is a simple example, not advice.

16
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:49,138
First, a reminder of the mortgage.

17
00:00:49,138 --> 00:00:53,333
Two hundred thousand pounds, over twenty-five years, at five per cent.

18
00:00:53,333 --> 00:00:57,528
That's one thousand, one hundred and sixty-nine pounds, eighteen pence

19
00:00:57,528 --> 00:01:00,885
a month, and about one hundred and fifty thousand, seven

20
00:01:00,885 --> 00:01:04,301
hundred and fifty pounds in interest over the whole term.

21
00:01:04,301 --> 00:01:07,477
In the early years, most of each payment is interest.

22
00:01:07,477 --> 00:01:09,875
Think of it as rent on the bank's money.

23
00:01:09,875 --> 00:01:12,692
You're paying to borrow it, not paying it back.

24
00:01:12,692 --> 00:01:15,928
It takes until month one hundred and thirty-five, more

25
00:01:15,928 --> 00:01:18,865
than eleven years in, before more of each payment

26
00:01:18,865 --> 00:01:20,902
goes to the loan than to interest.

27
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:25,356
So what does an extra hundred pounds actually do?

28
00:01:25,356 --> 00:01:30,302
In month one, the interest is eight hundred and thirty-three pounds, thirty-three.

29
00:01:30,302 --> 00:01:32,111
That's five per cent a year on

30
00:01:32,111 --> 00:01:34,524
two hundred thousand, split into twelve.

31
00:01:34,524 --> 00:01:37,238
The rest of the normal payment, three hundred

32
00:01:37,238 --> 00:01:40,616
and thirty-five pounds, eighty-five, pays down the loan.

33
00:01:40,616 --> 00:01:42,908
Add a hundred pounds, and four hundred

34
00:01:42,908 --> 00:01:46,165
and thirty-five pounds, eighty-five comes off instead.

35
00:01:46,165 --> 00:01:49,482
That extra hundred never earns the bank interest again.

36
00:01:49,482 --> 00:01:53,041
Next month's interest is forty-two pence lower, so a little

37
00:01:53,041 --> 00:01:55,997
more of your normal payment goes to the loan too.

38
00:01:55,997 --> 00:01:58,289
It's like a snowball rolling downhill.

39
00:01:58,289 --> 00:02:01,244
Small at first, but it picks up more every month.

40
00:02:02,740 --> 00:02:05,827
Keep that up every month, and here's where it lands.

41
00:02:05,827 --> 00:02:08,320
The mortgage is paid off after two hundred

42
00:02:08,320 --> 00:02:11,228
and fifty-eight months, instead of three hundred.

43
00:02:11,228 --> 00:02:13,009
Three and a half years sooner.

44
00:02:13,009 --> 00:02:15,918
That's forty-two monthly payments you never make.

45
00:02:15,918 --> 00:02:20,488
And the interest falls by twenty-four thousand, five hundred and five pounds.

46
00:02:20,488 --> 00:02:25,059
Over those years you put in twenty-five thousand, eight hundred pounds extra.

47
00:02:25,059 --> 00:02:27,196
That money still paid off your loan,

48
00:02:27,196 --> 00:02:28,798
and on top of it, you avoid

49
00:02:28,798 --> 00:02:31,766
more than twenty-four thousand pounds of interest.

50
00:02:31,766 --> 00:02:35,803
One hundred pounds a month is about three pounds, twenty-nine a day.

51
00:02:35,803 --> 00:02:36,812
Roughly a coffee.

52
00:02:36,812 --> 00:02:39,839
And twenty-four and a half thousand pounds is about

53
00:02:39,839 --> 00:02:41,976
the price of a good used family car.

54
00:02:43,480 --> 00:02:47,479
And remember month one hundred and thirty-five, when your payments

55
00:02:47,479 --> 00:02:50,689
finally start working more for you than for the bank?

56
00:02:50,689 --> 00:02:54,749
With a hundred pounds extra, that moment comes at month ninety-two.

57
00:02:54,749 --> 00:02:56,324
Nearly four years earlier.

58
00:02:56,324 --> 00:02:59,232
The rent on the bank's money shrinks faster, and

59
00:02:59,232 --> 00:03:02,079
every payment after that does more of the work.

60
00:03:03,580 --> 00:03:05,826
Here's the snowball in one picture.

61
00:03:05,826 --> 00:03:08,457
This is what you still owe, year by year.

62
00:03:08,457 --> 00:03:11,923
After five years, the extra hundred pounds a month has

63
00:03:11,923 --> 00:03:15,709
knocked six thousand, eight hundred pounds off the balance.

64
00:03:15,709 --> 00:03:18,597
After ten years, fifteen and a half thousand.

65
00:03:18,597 --> 00:03:22,062
After twenty years, the gap is over forty-one thousand

66
00:03:22,062 --> 00:03:25,335
pounds, and four years later, the mortgage is gone.

67
00:03:25,335 --> 00:03:27,838
The gap grows faster than the money you

68
00:03:27,838 --> 00:03:30,726
put in, because every pound you overpay stops

69
00:03:30,726 --> 00:03:33,934
costing you interest for every month that follows.

70
00:03:35,430 --> 00:03:36,756
Here's how it scales.

71
00:03:36,756 --> 00:03:40,921
Twenty-five pounds a month, about eighty-two pence a day, finishes

72
00:03:40,921 --> 00:03:44,204
a year early and saves around seven thousand pounds.

73
00:03:44,204 --> 00:03:47,296
Fifty pounds: one year, eleven months sooner, and

74
00:03:47,296 --> 00:03:50,642
thirteen thousand, four hundred pounds less interest.

75
00:03:50,642 --> 00:03:52,788
A hundred: three and a half years,

76
00:03:52,788 --> 00:03:55,060
and twenty-four and a half thousand.

77
00:03:55,060 --> 00:03:59,226
Two hundred: six years, two months, and nearly forty-two thousand.

78
00:03:59,226 --> 00:04:02,571
Three hundred pounds a month: eight years, two months

79
00:04:02,571 --> 00:04:06,359
sooner, and almost fifty-five thousand pounds less interest.

80
00:04:06,359 --> 00:04:09,199
Each extra pound saves a little less than the

81
00:04:09,199 --> 00:04:12,481
one before, because the loan ends sooner and there's

82
00:04:12,481 --> 00:04:14,817
less time left for interest to build.

83
00:04:14,817 --> 00:04:18,036
But even the smallest step makes a real difference.

84
00:04:19,540 --> 00:04:20,925
Quick question for you.

85
00:04:20,925 --> 00:04:23,634
Did you know you could overpay your mortgage?

86
00:04:23,634 --> 00:04:26,344
And has your lender ever told you your limit?

87
00:04:26,344 --> 00:04:27,789
Tell us in the comments.

88
00:04:27,789 --> 00:04:29,414
A simple yes or no is fine.

89
00:04:30,910 --> 00:04:33,072
Why does this matter more right now?

90
00:04:33,072 --> 00:04:36,435
Overpaying is a guaranteed saving at your mortgage rate.

91
00:04:36,435 --> 00:04:39,558
On a five per cent mortgage, every pound you overpay

92
00:04:39,558 --> 00:04:42,741
stops five per cent interest being charged on it, for

93
00:04:42,741 --> 00:04:45,383
every year it would have stayed on the loan.

94
00:04:45,383 --> 00:04:47,845
No risk, and no tax to pay on the saving.

95
00:04:47,845 --> 00:04:51,268
It's like a savings account that pays your mortgage rate.

96
00:04:51,268 --> 00:04:53,851
And after the Bank of England's rise on the

97
00:04:53,851 --> 00:04:57,094
fifth of November, many borrowers' rates have gone up,

98
00:04:57,094 --> 00:04:59,436
so each pound overpaid saves even more.

99
00:05:00,940 --> 00:05:02,242
What about a lump sum?

100
00:05:02,242 --> 00:05:05,143
Say after five years you have ten thousand pounds

101
00:05:05,143 --> 00:05:07,570
to spare, from a bonus or an inheritance.

102
00:05:07,570 --> 00:05:09,938
The balance by then is about one hundred

103
00:05:09,938 --> 00:05:13,193
and seventy-seven thousand, so ten thousand fits inside

104
00:05:13,193 --> 00:05:15,561
a typical ten per cent yearly allowance.

105
00:05:15,561 --> 00:05:17,692
You can usually choose what it does.

106
00:05:17,692 --> 00:05:20,297
Keep your payment the same, and the mortgage

107
00:05:20,297 --> 00:05:23,908
finishes twenty-two months early, with fifteen thousand, nine

108
00:05:23,908 --> 00:05:26,216
hundred and fifty pounds less interest.

109
00:05:26,216 --> 00:05:28,939
Or ask your lender to cut your monthly payment

110
00:05:28,939 --> 00:05:32,313
instead: about sixty-six pounds a month less, and roughly

111
00:05:32,313 --> 00:05:35,865
five thousand, eight hundred and forty pounds less interest.

112
00:05:35,865 --> 00:05:39,002
Shortening the term saves almost three times as much.

113
00:05:39,002 --> 00:05:42,436
Lowering the payment gives you breathing room every month.

114
00:05:42,436 --> 00:05:43,442
Neither is wrong.

115
00:05:43,442 --> 00:05:46,106
It depends on whether you need the money now.

116
00:05:47,600 --> 00:05:50,041
There's a bonus when your deal ends, too.

117
00:05:50,041 --> 00:05:52,541
A smaller balance means you're borrowing a

118
00:05:52,541 --> 00:05:54,624
smaller share of your home's value.

119
00:05:54,624 --> 00:05:56,172
That's your loan-to-value.

120
00:05:56,172 --> 00:05:59,327
Drop below a threshold, such as seventy-five or sixty

121
00:05:59,327 --> 00:06:02,184
per cent, and lenders often offer cheaper rates.

122
00:06:02,184 --> 00:06:04,863
So overpaying can lower the rate on your next

123
00:06:04,863 --> 00:06:07,244
deal, not just the interest on this one.

124
00:06:08,750 --> 00:06:10,760
Before you start, four checks.

125
00:06:10,760 --> 00:06:12,569
First, your lender's limit.

126
00:06:12,569 --> 00:06:15,651
Many fixed deals let you overpay up to ten per

127
00:06:15,651 --> 00:06:18,800
cent of the balance each year without a charge.

128
00:06:18,800 --> 00:06:21,883
Go over it, and you can pay an early repayment

129
00:06:21,883 --> 00:06:24,697
charge, often a few per cent of the extra.

130
00:06:24,697 --> 00:06:28,985
Trackers and standard variable rates often have no limit at all.

131
00:06:28,985 --> 00:06:30,392
Check your own terms.

132
00:06:30,392 --> 00:06:32,469
Second, keep an emergency fund.

133
00:06:32,469 --> 00:06:36,020
Money paid into the mortgage is hard to get back out.

134
00:06:36,020 --> 00:06:38,030
Think of it as a one-way door.

135
00:06:38,030 --> 00:06:40,174
Third, clear dearer debts first.

136
00:06:40,174 --> 00:06:42,720
A credit card at twenty per cent costs

137
00:06:42,720 --> 00:06:44,932
far more than a mortgage at five.

138
00:06:44,932 --> 00:06:48,081
And fourth, make sure the overpayment comes off

139
00:06:48,081 --> 00:06:50,761
the balance, not just your next payment.

140
00:06:50,761 --> 00:06:53,106
Some lenders need you to tell them.

141
00:06:54,600 --> 00:06:56,147
And it isn't a contract.

142
00:06:56,147 --> 00:07:00,207
On most mortgages, you can overpay one month and stop the next.

143
00:07:00,207 --> 00:07:03,236
Some flexible and offset mortgages even let you

144
00:07:03,236 --> 00:07:06,330
borrow overpayments back, or use your savings to

145
00:07:06,330 --> 00:07:08,908
cut the interest without paying them in.

146
00:07:08,908 --> 00:07:10,326
Ask what yours allows.

147
00:07:11,830 --> 00:07:13,259
So here's your next step.

148
00:07:13,259 --> 00:07:15,945
Log in to your lender's app or website, or give

149
00:07:15,945 --> 00:07:18,459
them a call, and ask what fifty or a hundred

150
00:07:18,459 --> 00:07:21,145
pounds a month extra would do to your end date.

151
00:07:21,145 --> 00:07:22,460
Most have a calculator.

152
00:07:22,460 --> 00:07:24,117
Then come back and tell us in

153
00:07:24,117 --> 00:07:26,746
the comments whether the answer surprised you.

154
00:07:28,240 --> 00:07:31,039
So, one hundred pounds a month on the mortgage

155
00:07:31,039 --> 00:07:33,837
from our first episode: three and a half years

156
00:07:33,837 --> 00:07:37,244
sooner, twenty-four and a half thousand pounds kept, and

157
00:07:37,244 --> 00:07:39,374
the flip nearly four years earlier.

158
00:07:39,374 --> 00:07:41,381
It's guaranteed, and it's simple.

159
00:07:41,381 --> 00:07:44,180
But is it the best use of that hundred pounds?

160
00:07:44,180 --> 00:07:47,709
Next week: overpay the mortgage, or invest the difference?

161
00:07:47,709 --> 00:07:49,838
There's one number that decides it.

162
00:07:49,838 --> 00:07:51,724
Subscribe so you don't miss it.
